  "query_preview": "..\n\nNoticeably warmer today amidst a decreasing cloud fraction and \nslowing northeasterly winds. The latter comes in response to the \nsurface pressure gradient becoming more diffuse over the Great \nLakes, positioned beneath the full-column Upper Midwest ridge that \nshould build northeastward to center over Ontario/Quebec. This also \naids in pushing out a meandering low-amplitude inverted shortwave \ntrough responsible for lingering mid/high cloud. Overlaying 500 mb \ngeopotential heights from the 00Z deterministic medium-range \nsolutions, Southeast Michigan situates along the interface between \nvicinity ridging and the western extent of a broad warm-core \nnor'easter, expected to stall over the lower Hudson Valley until \nMonday. The net result will be the advection of cool and dry air \nfrom Canada, yielding a rain-free forecast through the weekend for \nthe local area. The 26.00Z KDTX RAOB sampled this moisture-starved \nairmass between 20 kft AGL and yesterday's boundary-layer. With a \nmeasured PWAT of just 0.28 inches, a record low was observed for the \ndate/time pairing. Forecast soundings do indicate slight recovery in \ntropospheric moisture, with periods of high cloud today, and perhaps \na diurnal cumulus response Sunday afternoon. \n\nThe existing composite ridge axis extending from the Gulf into east-\ncentral Canada preserves surface high pressure into Monday and most \nof Tuesday next week. With little-to-no surface wind and 850 mb \ntemperatures holding near 10C, highs should easily reach the lower \n70s both days. The surface gradient could become reestablished as \nearly as Tuesday afternoon, taking on a south-southwesterly vector \nahead of the next precipitating system. A well-advertised, potent \nPacific longwave trough eventually digs onshore over California, \nabsorbing what becomes of eastern Pacific Hurricane Polo. This \nphased wave then ejects across The Plains, deflecting a lower \namplitude embedded northern stream shortwave over Lower Michigan \nlate Tuesday into Wednesday. Moisture advection from the southern \nsystem will lead to a marked uptick in ThetaE, eventually priming \nthe column for a few rounds of showers Wednesday and perhaps \nThursday. High variance exists amongst NWP QPF projections, affected \nby the differing track and evolution of the various competing \nsynoptic waves. Several deterministic solutions suggest little to no \nrainfall south of I-69, even through 00Z Friday (e.g. 00Z GEM, ICON, \nand UKMET).",
